# function size(view: Uint8Array) { return view.length; }   function count(table: Map<number, number>) { return table.size; }   sink(size(new Uint8Array(4)) + count(new Map())); new SharedArrayBuffer(4).grow;
# Link knows every caller, and what arrives stays as its class makes it. But reached code reads `grow` off shared memory, so the program may grow it, in another thread too,
# and a view that follows such memory is longer from then on. A read off a view that has neither a test nor a closed arm leaves a loop that runs no other code: `while (view.length === 4) { }` would spin for ever.
# So nothing is said of views there: the read is closed here, which runs once, and has a test and a closed arm in a loop: either holds it in its turn. A table follows no memory: it is still read without a test.
# RED UNTIL views-that-another-thread-may-lengthen-1: GetTypedArrayLength alone in `size`.
